The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and provides a drop-in `IFileSystem` interface.

### What to do

**1. Install the package:**
```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. For tests, also install:**
```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your class:**

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        // ...
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

**4. Register in DI:**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**5. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.Directory.CreateDirectory("/tmp/reports");
var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);

generator.GenerateReport("sales");

// Assert the file was written
var files = mockFs.Directory.GetFiles("/tmp/reports");
Assert.Single(files);
```

---

### Bonus: your class also has other statics

Looking at `ReportGenerator.cs`, I also see:

| Static | Recommended Abstraction |
|---|---|
| `DateTime.UtcNow` | **`TimeProvider`** (built-in on .NET 10) — register with `builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System)` |
| `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable` / `MachineName` | Custom **`IEnvironmentProvider`** wrapper |
| `Console.WriteLine` | Inject **`ILogger<T>`** instead |

Want me to refactor `ReportGenerator.cs` to use these abstractions?
